WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning, Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.100
I woke up in London a little bit jetlagged.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:03.730
All right, let’s go.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.420
The east end was also sleepy.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.880
Tattoo parlors were shuttered, the streets
almost empty.

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.429
On the walk to the tube, there was lots of
graffiti to enjoy.

00:00:10.429 --> 00:00:14.299
I made it to the station, stood on the right
for a very fast escalator ride, which turned

00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:19.559
out to be timed perfectly because there was
the train, and then boom we were in Trafalgar Square.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.020
There we found lots of tourists and statues
of kings and dandy lions and fashion models,

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.150
as well as this extraordinarily phallic thumbs
up sculpture.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.300
Mark was also filming all of this for The
Art Assignment, by the way, so soon there

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.340
will be an “Art Trip: London” video that’ll
be like this one, except much better.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:38.160
Headed over to the National Portrait Gallery,
where I saw Queen Elizabeth, Cardinal Wolsey,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.820
Thomas Cranmer, and many other people who
didn’t want you to know they had necks.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.340
I was really impressed by the museum, and
I found myself thinking that while portraits

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.320
are of course art works, they’re also historical
artifacts.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.600
Like even now, with images so easy to take
and store, the pictures we hold on to tell

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.300
us something about who and what mattered to
us.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:58.160
Got a bit to eat, then walked for a while
and started playing a game called

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.940
"Fancy Clock Has The Time Wrong.”

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.280
Eight minutes slow.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.399
Four minutes fast.

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:03.399
Not even close.

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:06.850
We then made our way to the Royal Academy
of the Arts, where an abstract expressionist

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.140
show was brilliant and also quite crowded.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:13.480
We then walked past Cupid and Shakespeare
getting crapped on by a pigeon to the Somerset House,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.960
which, as London real estate goes,
seems like a pretty nice house, aside from

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.640
the army of spoons in the courtyard.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.659
We saw an astonishing number of masterpieces
there, including this Seurat painting, and

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:27.670
this Cézanne landscape, where you can start
to see painting move toward abstraction.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.470
There was a very early Picasso, and this famous
painting by Degas, made around the Prussian

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.560
siege of Paris in 1871.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.990
Degas paid the model with a single hunk of
meat, which, according to the wall label,

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.320
she fell upon and devoured raw.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.380
There also Renoir and Pissarro, Van Gogh and
Manet, fancy clocks telling the wrong time,

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:50.000
a queen with her puppy, and this painting
by Goya, featuring the first recorded incidence

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.169
of man-spreading.

00:01:51.169 --> 00:01:53.049
After we left, I caught some Drowzees.

00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:56.460
London is lousy with Drowzees and masterpieces.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:02:00.600
Later that afternoon, we took the subway to
a gallery featuring this work form the Brazilian

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.140
artist Lygia Pape.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:06.380
The lines in this room, made by silver thread,
appear and disappear as you walk around them,

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.660
and it’s beautiful in a way the camera can’t
capture.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:14.440
The lines float and intertwine, responding
to the light and also to you.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:19.000
The artist Ad Reinhardt once described his
paintings as unmarketable, unphotographable,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.200
and unreproducible, and that’s how I felt
in this room.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:27.080
I can’t explain it to you, and I can’t
show it to you, because it’s unreproducible.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.069
These days, our experiences almost don’t
feel real if you can’t photograph them.

00:02:31.069 --> 00:02:36.629
At times, it feels like documenting a meaningful
experience is more important than having the experience.

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:40.300
It’s like “pics or it didn’t happen”
has actually become true.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.370
And even more weirdly, it feels to me sometimes
like maybe we do things for the pic.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:48.270
Like our choices are based on how well our
lives will photograph, rather than how those

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.230
lives actually make us feel.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:53.770
Like to use a tangential example, it’s hard
for me to know if I love this famous Van Gogh

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.599
self-portrait or if I just feel excited seeing
it in so-called “real life” after having

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:01.370
seen so many photographs and reproductions
of it.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:06.510
I guess I was thinking that looking at something
isn’t quite the same thing as looking at it through a lens.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.630
Of course, these aren’t entirely new questions.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:11.560
For one thing, this installation was first
made in 1977.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.379
But I think what I love so much about it is
that you couldn’t look at it through a lens.

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:17.099
You had to be there.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:22.059
So after a while, I put down my camera, and
I was just there.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:23.780
Hank, I’ll see you on Friday.

